     1	//! A utility verb's operand below its module, read as it was typed
     2	//! (cr-uv1-1).
     3	//!
     4	//! `rm`, `du`, `df` and `find` are the Unix commands run on a daemon's own
     5	//! disk, and to those commands HOW a path is written says something: `x/`
     6	//! names `x` only if it is a directory, and through a link; `x/.` likewise;
     7	//! `rm` refuses a path whose last name is `.` or `..`; a space at either
     8	//! end is part of a name. A path that was tidied on the way — its last
     9	//! separator dropped, a `.` folded away, its ends trimmed — is another
    10	//! path: `rm -rf HOST:/m/work/.` deleted `work`, `rm HOST:/m/doc/` unlinked
    11	//! a file, and of `report` and `report ` (a space after it) the wrong one
    12	//! went.
    13	//!
    14	//! So the path goes from the command line to the daemon as typed, and what
    15	//! its writing says is read here, by both ends, from that one text.
    16	//! Separators are `/`: a remote path is written with forward slashes on
    17	//! every machine.
    18	//!
    19	//! The text is BYTES (cr-uv1-4). A name on a Unix disk need not be UTF-8,
    20	//! and these commands print such a name by its own bytes; a path they
    21	//! print has to be one they take. [`Operand`] reads an operand from the
    22	//! command line so: the daemon and the module as text, the path below the
    23	//! module as the bytes typed.
